WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.400
Thank you to Target for supporting PBS.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.320
In 1519, the Bohemian Count Hieronymos Schlick
discovered a rich vein of silver near his

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.940
estate and decided to mint his own coins.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:19.250
Named after the valley they where were mined,
these Jaochimsthalers--or thalers for short--became

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:24.539
so popular around the world that the word
became synonymous with any big silver coin,

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:30.380
from the Italian tallero to the Ethiopian
talari to the American dollar.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.520
But why would an English colony use dollars
instead of the currency of its motherland:

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.520
pounds and shillings?

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.460
Well, during the 1700s, London was intent
on keeping as much gold and silver in the

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.760
country as possible.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.260
They actually outlawed the export of their
currency--even to their own colonies!

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.280
That meant that American colonists had to
rely on silver dollars from other countries--and

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.350
the most plentiful supply came from Mexico.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.880
Just south of the border, Spain was producing
vast amounts of silver coins at the Casa de

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.640
Moneda de Mexico, still operating today as
the oldest mint in the Americas.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.549
Officially called pesos, these silver coins
were worth eight reals, which is why you might

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:13.760
have heard pirates call them “pieces of
eight.”

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.760
(Ar.)

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.700
But to the American colonists, they were simply
dollars.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.249
No one knows for sure where the “dollar
sign” came from, but two

00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:24.890
of the leading theories involve the peso.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:31.890
One is that the abbreviation for it [PS] became
scrunched by hurried bookkeepers over time.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.110
Another suggests that the symbol refers to
an image on the back of the coin: a pillar

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.469
of Hercules with a banner wrapped around it
in an “S” shape.

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:42.899
Still, coinage in the colonies was hard to
come by.

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:48.729
What little there was went to Europe to pay
for imported goods, and it rarely came back.

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:55.100
In 1729, a young printer saw how a lack of
currency in Philadelphia was stifling the

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:01.079
local economy, prompting him to write an anonymous
pamphlet called A Modest Enquiry into the

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:03.659
Necessity of a Paper Currency.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:08.940
This was the same man responsible for many
famous financial quotes like “Time is money,”

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.390
and “nothing is certain but death and taxes.”

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.950
Yep, it was Benjamin Franklin.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.580
Franklin’s advocacy of paper currency earned
him contracts for printing money for the Pennsylvania

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.000
colony, but the English crown saw this as
a usurpation of its authority.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.200
By 1764, the British Parliament had outlawed
the use of paper money in America--just one

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.560
of many stubborn decisions that would eventually
provoke the colonies to rebellion.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:41.220
But to finance a Revolution, you need cash,
so the newly formed Continental Congress authorized

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.890
the printing of millions of dollars of Continental
Currency.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:50.890
Originally, these “continentals” were
valued at one to one Spanish dollar, but as

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.790
more and more were printed, their value fell
dramatically.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.050
If you’ve ever heard the saying, “Not
worth a continental,” that’s where it

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.050
came from.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.980
Though the American Revolution became the
first war financed and won with paper money,

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.670
the American people felt totally burned.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:11.650
The Founding Fathers decided to avoid the
controversy altogether by not mentioning paper

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.480
money in the Constitution at all, and it would
be almost a hundred years before the federal

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.830
government would print money again.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:24.980
With the Coinage Act of 1792, the United States
officially made the dollar the national currency.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.290
Instead of the confusing British system of
pounds, shillings, pence and farthings, the

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:35.820
dollar would be neatly divided into 100 equal
parts, which Thomas Jefferson suggested they

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.990
call “cents” after the Latin for “100.”

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:44.670
Thus, the American dollar became the first
fully decimal currency in the world, and the

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:50.170
new coins were struck in the first federal
building in America: the U.S. Mint in Philadelphia,

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.690
which predates even the White House and the
Capitol.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:57.460
Looking at these coins today, the first thing
that you might notice is what they’re missing:

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.820
famous people.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.320
America had just dispensed with monarchy and
elitism.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.650
The Founding Fathers wanted the images on
their money to represent ideas, not people.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.450
So when did that start to change?

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.370
The same time we started printing money again:
the Civil War.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:17.769
Before we get there, we should introduce an
important but little-known figure in American

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:20.230
history: Salmon P. Chase.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.100
Yes, that’s Salmon, like the fish.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.639
It’s biblical.

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:31.930
Salmon was an anti-slavery senator from Ohio
who earned his reputation as a lawyer defending

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:36.240
black fugitives from being shipped back to
their captors in the South.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.129
Salmon had his eye on the Oval Office for
a long time.

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:46.770
But in 1861, Salmon lost the Republican nomination
to the more moderate Abraham Lincoln, who

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.439
went on to win the presidency.

00:04:48.439 --> 00:04:54.080
As an olive branch to the radical wing of
the party, Lincoln made Salmon his Secretary

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.370
of the Treasury, and it was under Salmon that
the Treasury started printing paper money

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.469
again to finance the Union Army.

00:05:01.469 --> 00:05:06.590
As ambitious as ever, Salmon decided to put
the face of his ideal president on the first

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:09.389
American one dollar bill: his own!

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:15.199
The Treasury printed over $400 million worth
of paper notes, which came to be known as

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:17.350
“greenbacks,” because, well…

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:22.639
As with Continental Currency, greenbacks depreciated
as more were printed, at one point only worth

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:24.539
one-third their face value.

00:05:24.539 --> 00:05:28.270
As bad as that sounds, it was a lot worse
in the South.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.150
The Confederacy printed more than twice as
many of their Confederate State Dollars.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:39.349
The value dropped so precipitously that by
1865, a ten dollar Confederate note would

00:05:39.349 --> 00:05:42.449
only get you one or two cents worth of goods.

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:47.569
Incidentally, this bill printed in Louisiana
was commonly known as a Dixie, thanks to the

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:49.680
French word for “ten.”

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.940
Some people think that’s how the area around
New Orleans got the moniker “Dixieland,”

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.849
and how the South generally came to be known
as “the land of Dixie.”

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:03.259
Neither Dixies nor greenbacks could be exchanged
for gold and silver.

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:07.169
Their value was based on the promise that
the government would redeem them once their

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:08.770
side won the war.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:14.270
With the North’s decisive victory, Confederate
dollars were suddenly useless scraps of paper.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:20.210
But the greenback became such a trusted standard
that most people didn’t even bother redeeming

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.810
them, and America finally became a land of
federally regulated paper money.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:32.319
Ironically, in 1870, Salmon Chase, now a Supreme
Court Justice, claimed that the federal government

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:38.430
had no constitutional right to force people
to use paper money--effectively ruling against

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.240
his own actions as Secretary of the Treasury!

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:46.319
I’m sure it had nothing to do with the fact
that his face had recently been removed from

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:50.069
the one dollar bill and replaced with George
Washington’s.

00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:55.029
The ruling was overturned a couple years later,
but Salmon would eventually make his final

00:06:55.029 --> 00:07:02.309
mark on our currency, when in 1918 his portrait
was used on the $10,000 bill--the largest

00:07:02.309 --> 00:07:04.529
intended for general circulation.

00:07:04.529 --> 00:07:10.100
The next great leap forward for the dollar
would be thanks to, you guessed it, another war.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.059
America emerged from World War 2 as a major
global power.

00:07:18.059 --> 00:07:24.729
Its economy was perceived as so strong and
stable that, in 1945, the US dollar replaced

00:07:24.729 --> 00:07:28.900
the British pound sterling as the world’s
global reserve currency.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:34.809
Basically, this meant that banks and governments
around the world kept more US dollars on hand

00:07:34.809 --> 00:07:39.759
than any other foreign currency, and used
it as the metric for their own currency’s

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:40.759
value.

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:45.080
Throughout the 20th century, the connection
between gold and the dollar got weaker and

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:50.990
weaker until it was finally severed by the
Nixon Administration in 1971.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:56.869
The US dollar is now a fiat currency, meaning
its value is based only on the people’s

00:07:56.869 --> 00:07:58.409
faith in their government.

00:07:58.409 --> 00:08:03.839
While that sounds risky, it actually gives
Congress more power to deal with economic

00:08:03.839 --> 00:08:09.460
crises, as it did just this March by printing
extra money as a response to the coronavirus

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:10.460
pandemic.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.369
It’s probably not a coincidence that over
that time, we started to see more and more

00:08:14.369 --> 00:08:20.309
faces on our money, faces that inspire trust
and confidence in the American system.

00:08:20.309 --> 00:08:25.529
They’re mostly ex-presidents and Treasury
Secretaries (though not all!) and the final

00:08:25.529 --> 00:08:31.029
decision goes to the Secretary of the Treasury,
with the only guideline that they be “persons

00:08:31.029 --> 00:08:33.610
whose places in history the American people
know well.”

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:40.009
However, in honor of our anti-royal origins,
living people are prohibited from appearing

00:08:40.009 --> 00:08:41.399
on US currency.

00:08:41.399 --> 00:08:47.970
In 2016, the Treasury Department announced
that it would unveil a new $20 bill in 2020,

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:53.360
which would replace Andrew Jackson with famed
abolitionist Harriet Tubman, making her the

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.560
first African American historical figure to
appear on federal US currency.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:02.681
Don’t feel bad for Ol’ Hickory though--he
was vehemently opposed to paper money, so

00:09:02.681 --> 00:09:06.009
he probably wouldn’t’ve wanted his face
on the bill anyway.

00:09:06.009 --> 00:09:10.250
However, in 2019 the Trump Administration
announced that they would be delaying this

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.560
change by another 10 years at least.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:17.940
As Americans, we tend to take the dollar for
granted, almost like it’s an unchanging

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.100
natural phenomenon, like earth or air or water.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:26.899
But like any human tool, it was designed for
a purpose, and we constantly shape it to meet

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:28.149
the needs of the moment.

00:09:28.149 --> 00:09:34.440
It can cause stress and greed and misery,
but also hope and progress and prosperity.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:39.209
Without the dollar, we couldn’t have declared
independence or eradicated slavery.

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:42.709
The more we understand it, the better we can
control it.

00:09:42.709 --> 00:09:47.630
So maybe the next time you use a greenback,
you’ll think of the long journey it took

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.260
to end up in your pocket.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:51.310
And that’s our two cents!

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:55.670
We’d like to thank Target for being a proud
supporter of PBS.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:01.000
Since the first Target opened in 1962, their
